{
  "abstract": "Let $k \\geq 3$ and let $G$ be a biplanar graph that is not $k$-colourable, with the minimum number of vertices and a fixed planar partition of its edges. We show that every 3-cut $S$ of $G$ is independent and leaves exactly two components, and that the root relations of the two sides form the Latin pair $R_A = \\{E, \\mathrm{Rb}\\}$, $R_B = \\{P_{xy}, P_{xz}, P_{yz}\\}$. In each layer each side meets $S$ in a single rigid two-terminal piece, on different root pairs for the two sides. The proof combines single-layer planarity facts with an exact enumeration over abstract layer types; two independent implementations agree, and an ablation identifies the necessary rules. Consequences include root degree at least $k-1$ in each side and $e(K) \\leq 6|K| - 18$. The residual case is genuine: we construct biplanar graphs with an irreducible Latin 3-cut for every $k$ from 3 to 6, with 18, 16, 16 and 17 vertices, $(k+1)$-critical for $k = 5, 6$; with the critical examples of Paper 76 at $k = 7, 8$ this covers every $k \\leq 8$. So no argument using only three-root gadgets uniformly in $k$ can show that minimum counterexamples are 4-connected. At $k = 9$ a Latin pair would itself be a 10-chromatic biplanar graph, so the theorem turns minimum counterexamples with 3-cuts into one explicit construction template. For that case we show that the private part of any Latin side has chromatic number at least $k-2$, and that after peeling low-degree vertices each side of a Latin 3-cut at $k = 9$ has at least 11 private vertices of degree at least 9, by counting, a hand argument and exhaustive enumeration. Clique joins $K_j \\vee$ base with $j \\geq 3$ are excluded. Nothing here decides the Earth–Moon problem.",
